          title: `Teething chart: order and typical ages of the 20 baby teeth`,
          body: `Children have 20 primary (milk) teeth: 10 in the upper jaw and 10 in the lower jaw. The American Dental Association (ADA) eruption chart gives a typical age window for each tooth. These windows are averages from large groups of children, not deadlines. A tooth that comes a few months earlier or later than the chart is usually no cause for concern.\n\nThe most common order is: lower central incisors, upper central incisors, upper lateral incisors, lower lateral incisors, first molars, canines (eye teeth) and then second molars. Notice that the first molars usually arrive before the canines, which surprises many parents who expect the teeth to come in neatly from front to back. Teeth also tend to appear in pairs, one on each side of the jaw.`,
